e inserted onto the N or  the c-terminus of proteins that are  expressed in bacterial mammalian yeast  and insect cells as well as in vitro  expression systems the flag tag is  recognized by a high affinity rat  monoclonal antibody that is covalently  attached to our high capacity magnetic  agarose throughout the course of this  presentation I will demonstrate  effective automated purification of flag  tag proteins from multiple complex  biological systems focusing on three key  features that are most important to  researchers high yield high purity and  biological activity  you  table1 show  characteristics of the Pierce anti-flag  magnetic agarose the bees are composed  of magnetic highly cross-linked six  percent agarose they are ferromagnetic  with low remanence meaning that upon  release from a magnet the beads do not  maintain significant magnetism and can  be fully dispersed the bead diameter is  between 10 and 40 micron with an average  of about 25 micron  the bees are provided as a 25% slurry in  PBS containing point zero one percent  tween 20 along with sodium azide as a  preservative the binding capacity is  greater than or equal to 3 point 2  milligrams of flag tagged green  fluorescent protein or GFP per mil of  settled beads and lastly the flag tagged  proteins can be captured in as little as  20 minutes  one shows the purification of N and  c-terminal flag tagged sumo protein that  were overexpressed separately in e.coli  the proteins were captured with 12 and a  half microliters of settled anti-flag  magnetic agarose washed and then eluded  with 0.1 molar glycine with a pH of 2.8  sustained gel shows each lysate and the  purified proteins in duplicates  negligible nonspecific binding to the  bees is resulted in high purity protein  recoveries as shown  you  figure 2 shows the purification of 1x  and 3x flag tag tnf-alpha from XP Cho of  media each protein was expressed  separately in the xB Cho expression  system where they were secreted into the  media purification of the proteins was  performed in replicates of six on the  kingfisher flex instrument point four  mils of clarified media was incubated  with 50 microliters of settled beads for  one hour  the bees were washed and then eluded  with 0.1 molar glycine at pH 2.8 belowed  flow through zan delusions were  evaluated by stained sds-page in panel a  the left side of the gel shows minimal  to no protein remaining in the  flow-through  indicating efficient capture the right  side of the gel shows single equivalent  bands of eluded protein with no  background  you  protein yield was measured in each elias  by protein assays shown in panel B the  average amount of protein purified was  about a hundred and ten micrograms of  the One X flag tag tnf-alpha and 162  micrograms of three X flag teched-out  tnf-alpha the standard deviations were  less than three and corresponding CDs  were less than three percent it's  important to note that the difference in  protein yield is not reflective of the  difference in tag length but rather in  the difference in expression efficiency  each protein each purified protein was  further evaluated using a be a core  instrument and their binding kinetics  are shown in panel C flag antibody was  captured on a chip using a goat anti rat  AGG and then the purified proteins were  passed over the chip the data shows a  four-fold faster on rate or ka for the  3x tag but the off rate is similar for  both tags allowing for a 20 to 60 minute  bind or capture step there is  essentially no difference between the  one X and 3x flag tags at equilibrium  figure 3 shows the activity of the  purified 1x + 3 X flag tag tnf-alpha  HeLa cells were treated with and without  tag TNF alpha as well as TNF alpha  without a flag tag following 20 minutes  of treatment the cells were lysed and  partitioned into cytosol ik and nuclear  fractions the fractions were analyzed by  Western blot for the nuclear  transcription factor NF kappa-b P 65  which is known to translocate from the  cytosol into the nucleus upon cell  treatment with TNF alpha the treatments  are shown across the top of the block  and cytosol and nuclear fractions are  designated as C and n respectively in  the absence of TNF alpha nearly all of  the NF kappa-b is found in the cytosol  in contrast TNF alpha both with and  without a flag tag is shown to promote  transport of NF kappa-b into the nucleus  the data demonstrates that purification  with anti flag magnetic agarose  maintains the biological activity of  purified flag tag protein  figure 4 shows the purification of  c-terminal flag tagged green Rinella  luciferase expressed with a high yield  in vitro translation kit the protein was  immunoprecipitated with Pierce anti-flag  magnetic agarose and sigmaz anti-flag m2  magnetic beads on the Kingfisher flex  the bees were washed and then  competitively alluded with tree X flag  peptides panel a shows a Western blot of  the starting lysate  flow through L youit's and post Aleutian  B boils results for duplicate samples  are shown and all fractions were  normalized by volume significantly  higher yield was seen with a thermal  scientific anti-flag magnetic agarose to  evaluate nonspecific binding to the  beads the elements were resolved on a  gel and silver stained the only band  visible is the flag tagged green Rinella  luciferase indicating very low  background with both thermal and  competitor anti-flag magnetic beads  panel see shows the relative  fluorescence units of the eluded flag  tagged green rinoa luciferase  the amount of active protein seen with  the anti flag magnetic agarose is about  five times higher than competitor beads  corresponding to the Western blot  showing significantly more eluded  protein with a thermo scientific beads  overall the data shows that eluded  proteins maintains biological activity  throughout purification mass  spectrometry is a key proteomic  application that can be used to identify  multiple protein protein interactions in  a code immuno precipitation in Figure  five the terminal flag tagged bad  protein was transfected into HEC two  nine three cells and then that protein  was immunoprecipitated  from satellites aid with anti flag  magnetic agarose on a kingfish reflects  non transfected cells were used as a  control  annaleigh shows a Western blot of both  lysate and eluded protein that were  probed with anti bad antibody on the top  panel and anti-flag antibody on the  bottom bad is clearly seen in both life  state and LU 'it of transfected cells  but not in the control the le woods were  next analyzed by mass spectrometry to do  this the le wits were processed in  solution digested with trypsin and  analyzed by nano LC ms/ms using a thermo  scientific dye onyx nano LC system and a  qs active HS mass spectrometer the top  three peptides identified for the Lu it  from the lysate with expressed flag tag  bad correspondent to the bad protein the  non transfected control did not contain  any bad peptides  next protein-protein interactions were  identified using thermal scientific  proteome discover to point to software  and string database the bad protein as  well as multiple known interactors  including several isoforms of the  protein 14 3 3 and mitogen-activated  protein kinase 9 were identified panel D  shows the interactions of all of these  proteins with bad in a string diagram  you  figure six shows the immuno  precipitation of nanogram quantities of  flag tag protein that was spiked into a  mammalian cell lysate here twenty five  nanograms of n-terminal flag tagged  green fluorescent protein or GFP was  spiked  into a five four nine cell lysate and  captured using anti flag magnetic a  gross or Sigma's anti-flag m2 magnetic  beads using the Kingfisher flex tag  protein was alluded with point one molar  glycine at pH two point eight and  analyzed by Western blots going from  left to right the starting life state is  shown followed by duplicate Aleutians  and duplicate post dilution bead boils  yield of tagged GSP is seen to be higher  with thermal magnetic agarose verses  competitor beads indicating that the  higher capacity thermal beads are more  effective at capturing low abundant  targets  the Elliots were also run on a silver  stain gel to evaluate nonspecific  binding the only band visible is that of  the tag GFP with the thermo scientific  beads background for both thermal and  competitor beads is seen to be  negligible  you  in conclusion the Pierce anti-flag  magnetic aggros purifies biologically  active flag tag proteins with negligible  nonspecific background both the 1 X and  3x flag tag proteins are purified with  similar efficiency mass spectrometry can  be used to identify protein protein  interactions from immuno precipitations  and co-immunoprecipitation x' with  anti-flag magnetic agarose and lastly  both abundant and low abundance flag  tagged proteins ranging from nanogram up  to milligram quantities can be  efficiently purified from complex  biological systems with Pierce anti-flag  magnetic agarose  this concludes our presentation thank  you for your attention I'm happy to  answer your questions  Thank You dr. Benton's for that  informative poster presentation we'll  now start the live Q&amp;A portion of the  webinar if you have a question you'd  like to ask please do so now just click  on the ask a question drop-down box  located on the far left of your poster  presentation window type your question  into the box that appears on your screen  and click the send button  well answer as many of your questions as  we have time for now let's begin  first question is what are  benefits of using high-capacity magnetic  beads with Co IP  high-capacity beads work very well for  capturing low abundant targets you saw  that in the the last figure of the  presentation figure six  the reason for this is that do the  higher capacity you can capture more of  the available target in the lysate we've  seen higher yield in both Western blots  as well as in mass spectrometry of IP  life sites  you can also use in the case of magnetic  beads where say you have a higher  capacity than you might need you can use  less beads which will lower your cost  per sample and in addition if you can  use less beads potentially you can  reduce your background if you're working  with a particularly sticky sample  all right and can the flag tag be  removed yes the flag tag can most  definitely be removed and Terrell kinase  is the enzyme that can be used to remove  the flag tag on the n-terminus of a flag  tag protein the enzyme recognizes the  full flag tag the Dyk d4k sequence  cleaving after that last lysine residue  completely removing the tag from the  protein  again looks like we have one more  question where can I find Kingfisher  protocols for the anti flag magnetic  agarose our Kingfisher protocols are  these for the bees can be found on the  product page online at thermal Fisher  comm specifically if you're looking at  the product page for the anti flag  magnetic agarose you would go to the  bottom of that page under product  literature where that the links are  listed  you  well I would like to once again thank  dr. Benton for her poster presentation I  would also like to thank Thermo Fisher  Scientific for making today's  educational webcast possible before we  go I want to let everyone know that  today's webcast will be available for an  on-demand viewing through September of  2019 we share with your colleagues who  may have missed today's live event  that's all for now thank you for joining  us and we hope to see you again soon  goodbye

